  "prompt_text": "The figure is a simplified regional map showing the planned role of Bornholm as an “Energy Island” in the Baltic Sea between Denmark and Germany. Denmark is shaded in blue and Germany in red, with Bornholm highlighted to the east. The main idea is that offshore wind power generated near Bornholm would be collected at the island and then transmitted by high-voltage direct current (HVDC) links to the onshore electricity grids in both countries.\n\nTwo gold connection lines run from Bornholm Energy Island: one northwest toward the Danish coast and one south toward northern Germany. Each line ends at a labeled onshore grid connection point, emphasizing that the island serves as an offshore hub linking renewable generation to multiple national grids. Wind turbine symbols near Bornholm indicate the nearby offshore wind power plant feeding into this system.\n\nOverall, the image illustrates a cross-border energy infrastructure concept: Bornholm functions as a central node that integrates offshore wind generation and exports electricity to Denmark and Germany. The visual focus is on the geography of the connection routes and the island’s role as an intermediary between offshore renewable production and mainland power networks.",
